"Song structure is the arrangement of a song. It is typically sectional, which means a song uses repeating forms. In musical form, the first statement of a musical idea is called A. Later contrasting sections are labeled B, C, D, and so on. Musical form shows the importance of two principles: repetition and contrast. A verse of a song is a repeated sung melody where the words change from use to use. Each verse usually employs the same melody, while the lyrics usually change. A song structure in which all verses are sung to the same music is called strophic. Many folk and popular songs are strophic, including ballads and hymns. The chorus, or refrain, is the part of the song that repeats at least once, both musically and lyrically. It usually retains the same set of lyrics every time its music appears, and it is normally the most memorable part. Verse–chorus form has two main sections that often contrast in melody, rhythm, harmony, and loudness. The two words chorus and refrain are often used interchangeably. A bridge is a contrasting section that prepares for the return of the original material. It is a device used to break up the repetitive pattern of the song and keep the listener’s attention. The verse and the chorus are usually repeated throughout a song. The intro, the bridge, and the outro are usually used only once. An introduction generally has just music and no words. An outro is a way of finishing or completing the song. Pop songs may have an introduction and an ending, but these parts are not essential to the identity of most songs. The dictionary also lists the interlude: music inserted between the main sections of a song. There is no single order. Song form terminology is not standardized, and not every song has both a verse and a chorus. Some songs have only verses. Others, in the thirty-two-bar form, have an A section that shares the same melody three times with a contrasting B section. This is why we use the letters A, B, and C in this lesson, and not names. Can you hear what comes back and what is new?": "36f37ef8a818",
